
This is an experiment in assembly/C programming trying to
increase efficiency by reducing codesize or removing branches.

When converting a binary to hex string, I figured out that its
possible to do it with binary logic instead of if statements.

Normally the code would be (and the gcc compiler does a good job with);

	a = ... & 0xF
	if (a > 9)
		char = 'A' - 10 + a;
	else
		char = '0' + a;

x_libc_ptr64tohex:

	Just a simple wrapper for libc sprintf("%016lX")

c_noob_ptr64tohex:

	What a noob might make without using any lookup table

c_luta_ptr64tohex:

	Based on a lookup table instead

c_simp_ptr64tohex:

	Simple loop based non lookup

c_nobr_ptr64tohex:

	Branchless, all low nibbles then all high nibbles done at once

a_noob_ptr64tohex:

	Equivalent to c_noob slightly tweaked non lookup

a_bext_ptr64tohex:

	Loop 1 nibble at a time using bextr instruction

a_nobr_ptr64tohex:

	Assembly version of c_nobr
